  "query_preview": "/Through Saturday/...\nIssued at 109 PM MDT Sat Jul 25 2026\n\nIt's a hot day, with temperatures already in the 95-102F range. \nScattered thunderstorms will continue to develop over the higher \nterrain and spread eastward. Precipitable water values are\u00a0 0.90 \nto 1.25 (in) across the plains, so heavy rain is possible from the\nthunderstorms. Gusty outflow winds up to 50 mph are also possible\nnear the storms. The upper level high will remain centered over \nthe Central Rockies through Sunday. This will lead to a very warm \nnight and hot temperatures again Sunday, possibly a couple degrees\nwarmer. Some recorded highs today and Sunday will likely be tied \nor broken.\u00a0The airmass dries a little Sunday, but enough moisture \nwill be present for isolated to scattered thunderstorms. Coverage \nshould be slightly less than today's.\u00a0\n\nMonday's forecast will be more challenging and uncertain as a \nshortwave trough lifts northeast across Colorado while the center \nof the upper level high is nudged south. We'll see an increase in \nmoisture with precipitable water values climbing to around 1.25 to\n1.5 (in) across the plains, which is well above normal (~99th \npercentile at 1.5 (in)). The increased moisture and shortwave are \nexpected to bring cloud cover Monday morning and possibly showers.\nTemperatures will be cooler, but by how much depends how \nextensive the cloud cover ends up being. Instability may also be \nlimited if clouds linger for most/all of the day. However, the \nshortwave may help trigger showers and storms, making the forecast\na tricky one.\u00a0\n\nThe center of the high slowly shifts south and westward Tuesday \nand Wednesday, resulting in west to northwest flow aloft over \nColorado. Monsoonal moisture will be plentiful, with readings \ncontinuing to be well above normal. This is expected to produce \nscattered to numerous showers and thunderstorms Tuesday and \nWednesday. Temperatures will cool a little due to the increased \nmoisture and the high's center shifting southwest of the area. By \nThursday, the high is expected to be near the Four Corners. This \nis expected to continue the slightly cooler temperature trend with\nhighs in the upper 80s to mid 90s. The amount of moisture becomes\nless clear, though scattered showers and thunderstorms are still \nexpected.\u00a0 Northwest flow aloft continues for Friday and Saturday.\nModel consensus favors less moisture, which should result in \nfewer showers and thunderstorms.",
